\section{Introduction}


We would like to develop techniques to train AI agents that are helpful, honest, and harmless \cite{askell2021general}.  In this paper we show that we can train a relatively helpful and harmless\footnote{We do not focus explicitly on honesty/truthfulness in this paper, as we believe that techniques other than pure human feedback may be more efficient and effective at training models to be honest.  But we certainly believe that honesty is a crucial goal for AI alignment, and our models do improve on evaluations of honesty (see Figure \ref{fig:TruthfulQA}).} (HH) natural language assistant by collecting human preference data and applying the techniques of preference modeling (PMing) and reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF).  Our full training process is summarized in Figure \ref{fig:DiagramofTraining}.

\begin{figure}
