\subsection{Related Work}

Two recent papers, LaMDA \cite{LaMDA} and InstructGPT \cite{InstructGPT} have particular similarity to this work.  Both use human data to train large language models to be more generally useful or aligned.  Both use language models somewhat larger than our 52B model.

\subsection{Related Work}

Two recent papers, LaMDA \cite{LaMDA} and InstructGPT \cite{InstructGPT} have particular similarity to this work.  Both use human data to train large language models to be more generally useful or aligned.  Both use language models somewhat larger than our 52B model.

LaMDA \cite{LaMDA} finetunes large language models to participate in interesting, helpful, factually grounded, and safe natural language dialogue.  As with our work, they include notions of both positive interactions and safety/harmlessness.  And their use of external information to ensure accuracy/groundedness goes beyond the methods we discuss here, and is perhaps more similar to WebGPT and GopherCite \cite{WebGPT, GopherCite}.  However, some differences are that rather than using reinforcement learning, they apply a mix of supervised learning techniques (both generative and discriminative), and their data collection process involves absolute ratings rather than comparisons. They do not explore whether their methods impose an `alignment tax' on capabilities.

Our interest in studying trends with model size is motivated by neural scaling laws \cite{Hestness:2019:BHA:3293883.3295710, rosenfeld2019constructive, kaplan2020scaling}.  A related observation is that as parameter counts  grow, models finetune more effectively \cite{ScalingTransfer} and become much less vulnerable to `catastrophic forgetting'  \cite{Forgetting}.  We expect this effect helps to explain why our HH training is compatible with good evaluation performance and specialized skills for large models. 



