\subsection{Robustness Experiments}
\label{sec:RLHFRobustness}

We now discuss the problem of RLHF  robustness.  
A fully robust PM would agree with humans on distributions of dialogues quite different from those encountered during PM training (i.e. different from those created by crowdworker interactions with our deployed AI assistants).  However, we do not expect that our PMs are so robust, and in fact Figure \ref{fig:WorstPMPerformanceFromHHH} provides one plausible example of a robustness failure.  Since RL optimizes the policy to maximize the PM score, any failure in robustness on the part of the PM may be exploited by the RL policy to achieve higher rewards, without actually improving the policy's behavior from the point of view of human evaluators.

We provide more discussion in Appendix \ref{app:Robustness}.


