\paragraph{Few-shot outlier exposure}
Exposing the OOD detector to a few examples of the out-distribution has first been proposed by \cite{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.1812.04606}. \cite{thulasidasan2021a} suggested using a single class representing the OOD examples. \cite{fort2021exploring} showed that even strong near-OOD detectors enjoy a large benefit. 

Following the procedure from \cite{fort2021exploring}, we apply a single layer linear classifier on top of the activation vectors, while the rest of the language model is frozen. Given $M$ randomly drawn examples of the training set of the out-distribution (harmlessness data), we create a new binary classification problem, where the inputs are the combination of the full $N_\mathrm{train}$ examples of training set of the in-distribution with a target class of 0, and $N_\mathrm{train} // M$ copies of the $M$ examples of the out-distribution with the target class of 1. This ensure an equal representation of both in- and out-distribution examples and leads to better learning. Once we train the linear classifier, we use its class 1 output as the OOD score. The results are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:OOD_outlier_exposure}.


